## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for intent resolution, safe routing, and token budgeting under real-world noise.

## Key Concepts
- Intent detection and confidence thresholds
- Template routing to data-extraction, summarization, code-generation
- Safety guards to prevent system-prompt leakage
- Token budgeting and edge-case handling

## Code Example
```typescript
type Template = 'data-extraction'|'summarization'|'code-generation';
type Payload = { template: Template; tokenBudget: number; justification: string; };
function routePrompt(prompt: string, signals: {intent?: string; confidence?: number;}): Payload {
  const t = (signals.intent === 'summarize' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'summarization'
            : (signals.intent === 'extract' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'data-extraction'
            : (signals.intent === 'generate' && (signals.confidence ?? 0) > 0.8) ? 'code-generation'
            : 'summarization';
  const budgets: Record<Template, number> = {'data-extraction': 800, 'summarization': 500, 'code-generation': 1200};
  return { template: t, tokenBudget: budgets[t], justification: 'inferred from signals with fallback' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to n templates and multilingual prompts?
- How would you test guardrail effectiveness under prompt injections?

## Why This Is Asked

New angle: routing prompts to specialized engines in a multi-tenant setup, with strict isolation and auditable guardrails, mirrors real-world trading analytics workflows where latency and compliance are critical.

## Key Concepts

- Intent-driven routing
- Per-engine schemas and validation
- Tenant isolation and leakage prevention
- Tamper-evident auditing with cryptographic hashes

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype: routePrompt(input) -> {engine, payload}
function routePrompt(input){
  const t = inferIntent(input); // Analytics|Risk|Compliance
  const engine = {analytics:'Analytics', risk:'Risk', compliance:'Compliance'}[t];
  const payload = validateAgainstSchema(engine, input);
  const audited = {id: Math.random().toString(36).slice(2), engine, input, timestamp: Date.now()};
  audited.hash = require('crypto').createHash('sha256').update(JSON.stringify(audited)).digest('hex');
  return {engine, payload, audited};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle prompts that imply multiple engines?
- What trade-offs exist between strict schemas and user flexibility in finance contexts?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design guardrails early in prompt pipelines, focusing on simple heuristics, mitigations, and verifiable safety.

## Key Concepts

- Risk scoring from prompts
- PII and system-prompt leakage detection
- Mitigations: redaction, output limits, guardrails
- Guarded prompts and verification

## Code Example

```javascript
function riskScore(prompt) {
  let score = 0;
  if (/\b(email|e-mail|phone)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 3;
  if (/\b(export|download|CSV|exported)\b/i.test(prompt)) score += 2;
  if (prompt.toLowerCase().includes("system prompt")) score += 4;
  return Math.min(score, 10);
}
function mitigate(prompt) {
  return prompt
    .replace(/[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/g, "[REDACTED_EMAIL]")
    .replace(/\b\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{3}[-.\s]?\d{4}\b/g, "[REDACTED_PHONE]");
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for multi-tenant workloads with different policy baselines?
- What tests would you add to validate redactions without affecting legitimate outputs?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design a practical, testable clarifier routing mechanism, not just theory.

## Key Concepts
- Rule-based routing and simple scoring
- Signals: intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, conflicts, missing data
- Payload design for downstream modules

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny prototype
function chooseClarifier(signals){
  const {intent_confidence, data_availability, user_role} = signals;
  if (intent_confidence < 0.5) return 'request-region';
  if (data_availability.includes('period')) return 'request-period';
  if (user_role==='analytics') return 'request-metric';
  return 'request-period';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multilingual prompts?
- How would you evaluate clarifier quality in automated tests?

## Why This Is Asked

Real-world fintech data assistants service multiple tenants; the router must enforce per-tenant policies, guard against data leakage, and handle conflicting signals and latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-aware policy retrieval and governance
- Guardrails and data-redaction
- Edge-case handling: conflicting signals, missing policy, leakage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-router sketch
async function routePrompt(prompt, tenantId){
  const policy = await fetchPolicy(tenantId);
  if(!policy) throw new Error('Missing policy');
  if(!policy.allow(prompt)) throw new Error('Action not allowed');

  const intent = analyzeIntent(prompt);
  const module = selectModule(intent, policy);
  const plan = generatePlan(module, intent);

  return { module, plan, payload: buildPayload(module, prompt) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Gauges practical, client-side prompt evaluation with safety and tone controls for high-stakes brands. Focuses on a beginner-friendly rubric and a concrete routing prototype, suitable for scalable chat systems.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt scoring rubrics
- Client-side routing logic
- Safety and bias considerations
- Minimal unit testing for NLP-ish logic

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(input){
  const lower = input.toLowerCase();
  const clarity = /order|status|refund/.test(lower) ? 4 : 2;
  const safety = !lower.includes('password') ? 4 : 2;
  const bias = /(racial|gender|biased)/.test(lower) ? 5 : 1;
  if (clarity >= 4 && safety >= 4 && bias <= 2) return 'concise';
  if (lower.includes('please') || lower.includes('sorry')) return 'empathetic';
  return 'authoritative';
}
```

